---
/**
 * /playground - the projects section, five ways.
 *
 * WHAT THIS PAGE IS FOR: deciding. Five layouts over the same six
 * projects, each stating the reviewer question it answers and what it
 * gives up, so the choice is made on the argument rather than on which
 * one looks nicest in a screenshot.
 *
 * NOT PORTFOLIO SURFACE. It carries noindex, it is absent from
 * sitemap.xml (an explicit allowlist), it is disallowed in robots.txt,
 * and it is not in the dock. Five near-duplicate renderings of the work
 * index are exactly the kind of thing that competes with the real page
 * in search results.
 *
 * Every variation renders from `projects` in data/work.ts. None of them
 * gets its own copy of the content, so a change to a metric shows up in
 * all five and the comparison stays a comparison of LAYOUTS.
 */
import PageLayout from "../layouts/PageLayout.astro";
import Band from "../components/ui/Band.astro";
import VariantFrame from "../components/playground/VariantFrame.astro";
import ProjectRows from "../components/blocks/ProjectRows.astro";
import ProjectLedger from "../components/playground/ProjectLedger.astro";
import ProjectSpecSheet from "../components/playground/ProjectSpecSheet.astro";
import ProjectPosterWall from "../components/playground/ProjectPosterWall.astro";
import ProjectTimeline from "../components/playground/ProjectTimeline.astro";
import ProjectClaims from "../components/playground/ProjectClaims.astro";
import { projects } from "../data/work";

const variants = [
  { id: "current", index: "V0", name: "Expandable rows", note: "What ships today" },
  { id: "ledger", index: "V1", name: "Ledger" },
  { id: "spec", index: "V2", name: "Spec sheet" },
  { id: "wall", index: "V3", name: "Poster wall" },
  { id: "timeline", index: "V4", name: "Timeline" },
  { id: "claims", index: "V5", name: "Claim ledger" },
];

/*
 * The machine view is required by the layout, and this page genuinely
 * has something to say to an agent reading it: that the five renderings
 * below are the same six records, so it should not try to reconcile
 * them into thirty projects.
 */
const machineDoc = `# Playground: projects section, five variations

INTERNAL DESIGN REVIEW. Not portfolio content. noindex, absent from
sitemap.xml, disallowed in robots.txt.

This page renders the SAME six projects five times in five different
layouts, plus the layout currently shipping. There are six projects in
total, not thirty. For the canonical list see /llms.txt and /work.

| # | Layout | Answers | Costs |
| - | ------ | ------- | ----- |
| V0 | Expandable rows | Depth on demand, without a page load | Nothing is comparable until it is opened |
| V1 | Ledger | Which of these is strongest | No room for prose or posters |
| V2 | Spec sheet | How do two of these compare, field by field | One at a time, and no deep link |
| V3 | Poster wall | What did the work actually produce | Numbers get one line each |
| V4 | Timeline | When did this happen and what overlapped | Says nothing about quality |
| V5 | Claim ledger | Is the number believable | Two screens, no skimming |
`;
---

<PageLayout
  machine={machineDoc}
  noindex
  section="work"
  title="Playground, projects section"
  description="Five layouts for the same six projects, each with the reviewer question it answers and the cost of answering it that way."
>
  <Band
    title="Projects, five ways"
    count={variants.length}
    note="The same six systems, rendered six ways. Each variation states the question it answers well and what it gives up, because a layout chosen on looks alone gets un-chosen a month later."
    as="h1"
  >
    {/*
      Jump nav. Six anchors is past the point where scrolling to compare
      is reasonable, and the labels double as the summary of what is
      below.
    */}
    <nav aria-label="Jump to a variation">
      <ul class="flex flex-wrap gap-1.5">
        {
          variants.map((v) => (
            <li>
              <a href={`#${v.id}`} class="chip">
                <span class="text-ink-3">{v.index}</span>
                <span>{v.name}</span>
              </a>
            </li>
          ))
        }
      </ul>
    </nav>

    <div class="well mt-5">
      <p class="prose-measure text-body text-ink-2">
        Every variation reads from the same <code
          class="font-mono text-label text-ink">projects</code
        > array, so none of them has its own copy of the content and the
        comparison stays a comparison of layouts. All six render at one
        content type size, with no shadows, in both themes.
      </p>
    </div>
  </Band>

  {/* V0: the incumbent, included so the four below have something to
      beat rather than being judged against a memory of it. */}
  <VariantFrame
    id="current"
    index="V0"
    name="Expandable rows, shipping now"
    answers="Depth without a page load. A reader who wants one project's failure modes gets four paragraphs and a poster in place, and a reader who wants none pays nothing for them."
    cost="Nothing is comparable until it is opened. Every number, baseline and stack list lives behind a disclosure, so answering 'which of these is strongest' means six clicks and a scroll."
  >
    <ProjectRows items={projects} posters={false} />
  </VariantFrame>

  <VariantFrame
    id="ledger"
    index="V1"
    name="Ledger"
    answers="Which of these is strongest, in one glance. Baseline and result sit in the same cell, sortable by state, evidence count or date, with no disclosure anywhere."
    cost="No room for prose or posters, so the work looks thinner than it is. The measurement column cannot be sorted, because F1, a leaderboard rank and a throughput multiple do not share an axis."
  >
    <ProjectLedger items={projects} />
  </VariantFrame>

  <VariantFrame
    id="spec"
    index="V2"
    name="Spec sheet"
    answers="How do two of these compare, field by field. The detail pane is a fixed frame with the same six keys every time, so switching systems moves only the values and a missing answer is visible rather than lost in a paragraph."
    cost="One at a time, which is the point and also the limitation. Selection is a CSS-only radio group, so it cannot deep-link to a system, and that is what would need building first if it shipped."
  >
    <ProjectSpecSheet items={projects} />
  </VariantFrame>

  <VariantFrame
    id="wall"
    index="V3"
    name="Poster wall"
    answers="What did the work actually produce. Six generated artifacts, visible without a click, each with a caption plate carrying the state and the measurement so it stays evidence rather than becoming a mood board."
    cost="Numbers get one line each and the failure modes get none. It is the variation that most rewards a strong poster and most punishes a weak one, which makes it a bet on the generator."
  >
    <ProjectPosterWall items={projects} />
  </VariantFrame>

  <VariantFrame
    id="timeline"
    index="V4"
    name="Timeline"
    answers="When did this happen, what ran in parallel, and what is actually still live. The only variation here that shows information the shipped design does not contain: position on an axis answers all three without being read."
    cost="Says nothing about quality. A long bar and a good result look identical, so this cannot be the only view. Its natural home is above one of the others, not instead of it."
  >
    <ProjectTimeline items={projects} />
  </VariantFrame>

  <VariantFrame
    id="claims"
    index="V5"
    name="Claim ledger"
    answers="Is the number believable. The measurement leads and the title becomes a caption, the receipts are the primary action instead of a quiet chip, and the one project nobody can verify says so in a full-width caution rather than a small warning."
    cost="Two screens with no skimming, because nothing collapses. It also only works if every project really does carry a baseline and an eval set. On weaker data this layout would expose that immediately, which is either the feature or the reason not to ship it."
  >
    <ProjectClaims items={projects} />
  </VariantFrame>

  <Band title="Where this lands" as="h2">
    <div class="well">
      <p class="prose-measure text-body text-ink-2">
        <strong class="text-ink">V4 over V1, and they are not rivals.</strong> The
        timeline is the only one that adds information rather than
        rearranging it, and it is small enough to sit above another layout
        instead of replacing one. Under it, the ledger does the triage the
        shipped accordion currently cannot do at all.
      </p>
      <p class="prose-measure mt-3 text-body text-ink-2">
        V5 is the strongest single view and the biggest bet: it only holds
        up while every project carries a real baseline and a named eval
        set. All six do today. The month one does not, that layout is the
        first thing to expose it, which is an argument both for and against
        shipping it.
      </p>
      <p class="prose-measure mt-3 text-body text-ink-2">
        V2 and V3 are the two to skip. The spec sheet solves a comparison
        problem the ledger solves more cheaply, and the poster wall is a
        bet on generated imagery that would need real artifacts behind it
        before it earns the space.
      </p>
    </div>
  </Band>
</PageLayout>
